WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.310 --> 00:00:06.629
Love it or leave it, Suicide Squad ushered
a ton of DC Comics' zaniest characters onto

00:00:06.629 --> 00:00:12.040
the big screen for the first time—and gave
each one a makeover in the bargain.

00:00:12.040 --> 00:00:17.419
While some stayed closer to their comic appearances
than others, there were a few Squad members

00:00:17.419 --> 00:00:19.439
who were left completely transformed.

00:00:19.439 --> 00:00:23.720
Here's how the Suicide Squad should really
look...

00:00:23.720 --> 00:00:25.050
Captain Boomerang

00:00:25.050 --> 00:00:29.489
Just like in the comics, Captain Boomerang
is not really an upstanding guy.

00:00:29.489 --> 00:00:34.550
Aesthetically, though, the Captain's live-action
portrayal diverges significantly from his

00:00:34.550 --> 00:00:35.650
original form.

00:00:35.650 --> 00:00:41.780
Now there are probably no more ridiculous
outfits than his original boomerang-print

00:00:41.780 --> 00:00:46.980
tunic and tiny hat, so new Boomerang is a
welcome departure.

00:00:46.980 --> 00:00:51.920
The on-screen version is based more closely
on Boomer’s modern costume, so they both

00:00:51.920 --> 00:00:57.680
wear a similar grey-blue peacoat and both
have a knack for throwing boomerangs...which,

00:00:57.680 --> 00:01:01.100
for the record, are the worst weapons of all
time.

00:01:01.100 --> 00:01:03.800
I can't believe I said that as an Australian.

00:01:06.640 --> 00:01:07.280
"Oh no!

00:01:07.280 --> 00:01:08.640
It's coming back this way!"

00:01:08.640 --> 00:01:12.780
"That throwing stick stunt of yours has boomeranged
on us!"

00:01:12.780 --> 00:01:16.130
But Jai Courtney's version has urbanized clothing
and jewelry.

00:01:16.130 --> 00:01:22.350
His hair is not the orange of his comics counterpart,
but he does occasionally wear the knit cap.

00:01:22.350 --> 00:01:26.850
Courtney also extended the character's usual
sideburns into fuller facial hair and snuck

00:01:26.850 --> 00:01:29.020
in a little eye makeup to boot.

00:01:29.020 --> 00:01:30.509
El Diablo

00:01:30.509 --> 00:01:37.070
The fiery essence of Chato Santana, or El
Diablo, was definitely not lost in translation,

00:01:37.070 --> 00:01:40.389
but there were still some noticeable style
changes with his ink.

00:01:40.389 --> 00:01:44.810
In the comics, he has the swirl designs that
emphasize his flame theme.

00:01:44.810 --> 00:01:49.840
With Jay Hernandez in the live-action role,
the character’s skin art is changed to a

00:01:49.840 --> 00:01:54.429
scattered slew of new symbols that paint him
as a true tough guy.

00:01:54.429 --> 00:02:00.270
Among his new markings are hash marks on his
eyebrows and a skeletal teeth design featured

00:02:00.270 --> 00:02:05.299
on his upper lip, as well as a grim reaper
scythe on his forehead to show he's in the

00:02:05.299 --> 00:02:10.530
business of death-dealing, even whilst on
his journey of repentance.

00:02:10.530 --> 00:02:11.599
Katana

00:02:11.599 --> 00:02:17.120
Superheroine Katana has worn multiple costumes
over her various TV appearances.

00:02:17.120 --> 00:02:24.260
But when she first appeared in comics in 1983,
she had a red-and-yellow costume with ridiculous,

00:02:24.260 --> 00:02:26.780
pointed shoulders and fur-topped shoes.

00:02:26.780 --> 00:02:31.540
You know, the least stealthy martial arts
costume possible.

00:02:31.540 --> 00:02:37.870
Over time, she toned it down into a red-and-gold
armor deal with a domino mask, but it’s

00:02:37.870 --> 00:02:42.680
Katana’s completely revamped New 52 look
that appeared on screen.

00:02:42.680 --> 00:02:47.799
Karen Fukuhara's rendition of Katana still
changes things up in a few key ways.

00:02:47.799 --> 00:02:53.769
Her screen costume is a bit more conservative
than the skin-tight leotards Katana wears

00:02:53.769 --> 00:02:58.969
in the comics, instead opting for a loose-fitting
plain t-shirt and pants.

00:02:58.969 --> 00:03:04.019
She also doesn’t have traditional samurai
shoulder guards, instead wearing a varsity-style

00:03:04.019 --> 00:03:07.180
jacket featuring floral tributes to her heritage.

00:03:07.180 --> 00:03:11.219
Even with these changes, Katana is still pretty
recognizable.

00:03:11.219 --> 00:03:12.420
Slipknot

00:03:12.420 --> 00:03:17.390
One of the most significant design departures
in Suicide Squad can be found in Adam Beach's

00:03:17.390 --> 00:03:19.230
version of Slipknot.

00:03:19.230 --> 00:03:25.030
The movie's costumes crew almost totally dumped
the character's comics appearance: a full,

00:03:25.030 --> 00:03:30.799
muscle-hugging bodysuit with bizarre side
windows, loose sleeves, and a mask.

00:03:30.799 --> 00:03:34.609
In the movie, Slipknot got a much more militaristic
suit.

00:03:34.609 --> 00:03:37.659
Gone was the corny noose design on his chest.

00:03:37.659 --> 00:03:43.750
Instead, he was decked out in useful utility
gear and a built-in rappelling harness.

00:03:43.750 --> 00:03:46.920
And his hair was tied back to look like—what
else?—ropes.

00:03:46.920 --> 00:03:49.780
Can’t say the guy doesn’t have a theme.

00:03:49.780 --> 00:03:52.879
Too bad he was only on-screen for about three
minutes.

00:03:52.879 --> 00:03:54.069
Rick Flag

00:03:54.069 --> 00:03:59.290
Joel Kinnaman's interpretation of Rick Flag
is relatively faithful to the modern version

00:03:59.290 --> 00:04:03.510
of the character, but there are still a handful
of small style changes.

00:04:03.510 --> 00:04:08.890
As a member of the U.S. Army's Special Forces,
the comics version of Rick Flag keeps a close

00:04:08.890 --> 00:04:13.959
shave most of the time, whereas the movie
version has a little more going on under his

00:04:13.959 --> 00:04:14.959
nose.

00:04:14.959 --> 00:04:19.919
Kinnaman's also a living, breathing human,
so he’s a little less beefy than the Flag

00:04:19.919 --> 00:04:22.160
familiar to DC readers.

00:04:22.160 --> 00:04:27.310
However, just like his paper-bound predecessor
in the role, Kinnaman's take tended to wear

00:04:27.310 --> 00:04:31.419
the same Army green jumpsuit and battlefield-grade
arms.

00:04:31.419 --> 00:04:37.180
And of course, the low-guard buzz cut that
serves as a not-so-subtle salute to his military

00:04:37.180 --> 00:04:38.310
service.

00:04:38.310 --> 00:04:39.990
Amanda Waller

00:04:39.990 --> 00:04:46.500
Just as in the comics, Amanda Waller is the
tough assembler of Task Force X who, in actress

00:04:46.500 --> 00:04:53.560
Viola Davis' words, is a "big powerful black
woman, hard, ready to pick up a gun and shoot

00:04:53.560 --> 00:04:54.650
anyone at will."

00:04:54.650 --> 00:05:00.650
Unlike many of the other characters in Suicide
Squad, Davis' version of Waller bypassed DC

00:05:00.650 --> 00:05:06.360
Comics' rebooted New 52 rendition of her character
and went all the way back to her earlier,

00:05:06.360 --> 00:05:10.949
more business-oriented look, like in her 1980s
Legends debut.

00:05:10.949 --> 00:05:17.710
Her pearls and hairdo, for example, were pretty
close replicas of some of her earliest appearances.

00:05:17.710 --> 00:05:18.710
The Joker

00:05:18.710 --> 00:05:25.860
While Jared Leto's version of the Joker adopted
some of the comics' version’s physical characteristics,

00:05:25.860 --> 00:05:30.210
it was also a new take on the character in
several noticeable ways.

00:05:30.210 --> 00:05:35.470
While Leto did keep the Joker's bright green
'do, he slicked it down, instead of the more

00:05:35.470 --> 00:05:39.139
unkempt approach often favored by the classic
Joker.

00:05:39.139 --> 00:05:44.880
Leto's Joker ushered in the era of the character
with grilled teeth and threatening tattoos.

00:05:44.880 --> 00:05:49.330
And while we don’t get to see Joker’s
bare back, set photos and statues based on

00:05:49.330 --> 00:05:55.289
the movie revealed a dragon tattoo that looks
pretty similar to the one Joker has in All-Star

00:05:55.289 --> 00:05:56.940
Batman and Robin.

00:05:56.940 --> 00:06:00.539
Even his signature purple coat got a biker-friendly
update.

00:06:00.539 --> 00:06:06.330
Rather than the playful suit styling of old,
he had a shiny alligator skin number and went

00:06:06.330 --> 00:06:11.900
shirtless beneath, just in case people wanted
to see that he wrote his own name on his stomach.

00:06:11.900 --> 00:06:16.660
You know...just in case he forgot, or got
lost at the mall.

00:06:16.660 --> 00:06:18.349
Harley Quinn

00:06:18.349 --> 00:06:24.669
Margot Robbie hit a home run as the bat-wielding
Harley Quinn, but the character is hardly

00:06:24.669 --> 00:06:29.800
recognizable as the one that debuted in Batman:
The Animated Series.

00:06:29.800 --> 00:06:35.250
The old Harley Quinn looks like a straight-up
jester, which we got to see onscreen during

00:06:35.250 --> 00:06:40.139
the Suicide Squad for one hot second, but
it's the later versions of the character that

00:06:40.139 --> 00:06:43.270
lent a great deal to Harley’s movie look.

00:06:43.270 --> 00:06:49.190
Robbie is a postmodern punk complete with
colorful pigtails, messy makeup, tattoos,

00:06:49.190 --> 00:06:53.050
and a get-up that should appeal to cheerleaders
and biker chicks alike.

00:06:53.050 --> 00:06:58.610
The pigtails are borrowed specifically from
Harley’s appearance in the video game Batman:

00:06:58.610 --> 00:07:03.659
Arkham City, and the tattered t-shirt is pure
Injustice: Gods Among Us.

00:07:03.659 --> 00:07:07.840
And we can thank her New 52 solo title for
those shorts.

00:07:07.840 --> 00:07:09.110
Killer Croc

00:07:09.110 --> 00:07:14.509
The appearance and origin story of Killer
Croc have changed so many times over the years

00:07:14.509 --> 00:07:17.289
that there’s really not one “true” Croc.

00:07:17.289 --> 00:07:22.720
But the guy we see in Suicide Squad is surprisingly
true to his very first full appearance in

00:07:22.720 --> 00:07:27.449
Batman #358, where he’s just a guy with
a skin condition.

00:07:27.449 --> 00:07:32.610
During his first appearance, he showed up
in a standard mobster trenchcoat-and-hat ensemble.

00:07:32.610 --> 00:07:37.449
In Suicide Squad, he’s wearing a hoodie,
which is pretty much the modern equivalent.

00:07:37.449 --> 00:07:43.050
As time goes by in the comics, Croc slowly
devolves into a more brutish, animalistic

00:07:43.050 --> 00:07:48.479
villain, sprouting a tail and long reptilian
snout, so while the cinematic counterpart

00:07:48.479 --> 00:07:53.940
seems to be a relatively regular guy now,
the future isn’t looking too bright.

00:07:53.940 --> 00:07:55.639
Sorry, dude.

00:07:55.639 --> 00:07:56.650
Deadshot

00:07:56.650 --> 00:08:01.970
It's hard to hide a face as marketable as
Will Smith's, so it's no wonder the Suicide

00:08:01.970 --> 00:08:06.349
Squad version of Deadshot doesn't often wear
his trademark mask.

00:08:06.349 --> 00:08:11.460
When he does put it on, the on-screen version
flips the script and places his fancy sight

00:08:11.460 --> 00:08:15.340
over Will’s left eye, whereas comics Deadshot
favors the right.

00:08:15.340 --> 00:08:19.169
It’s a relatively small difference compared
to the big picture, however.

00:08:19.169 --> 00:08:25.250
On screen, he wears a uniform that looks like
riot gear, and his usual red-and-silver costume

00:08:25.250 --> 00:08:26.659
is nowhere to be seen.

00:08:26.659 --> 00:08:32.320
Also, Floyd Lawton has traditionally been
a white guy with a doofy little mustache.

00:08:32.320 --> 00:08:35.980
Honestly, we prefer the Fresh Prince in this
instance.

00:08:35.980 --> 00:08:38.330
(rad Fresh Prince snap)

00:08:38.330 --> 00:08:43.979
All of this, however, is light years away
from Deadshot’s first appearance in 1950,

00:08:43.979 --> 00:08:47.870
where he looked like a stage magician, top
hat and all.

00:08:47.870 --> 00:08:53.870
He tangled with Batman once and disappeared
until 1977, when he took on his more modern

00:08:53.870 --> 00:08:56.620
look...which is still nothing like what we
see on screen.

00:08:56.620 --> 00:08:58.140
Enchantress

00:08:58.140 --> 00:09:03.040
In the comics, Enchantress receives her powers
after wandering off into a creepy room at

00:09:03.040 --> 00:09:09.380
a mansion party and talking to a fat magic
head in a giant chair, because why not?

00:09:09.380 --> 00:09:15.610
After blonde, bob-haired June Moone transforms
into Enchantress, she looks like a standard

00:09:15.610 --> 00:09:21.980
sexy Halloween witch: green pointed hat, long
cape, miniskirt, and thigh-high boots.

00:09:21.980 --> 00:09:27.860
Later, as she loses control of her powers
and turns a little evil, she dons an emerald

00:09:27.860 --> 00:09:33.890
green halter top and gold band fitted around
her head, with occasional returns to her old-school

00:09:33.890 --> 00:09:34.890
look.

00:09:34.890 --> 00:09:40.450
In Suicide Squad, Enchantress is an archaeologist
who gets her powers from an ancient relic.

00:09:40.450 --> 00:09:46.250
Whatever got under her skin in that creepy
old temple also gave her a complete, gross

00:09:46.250 --> 00:09:51.560
makeover, making her far more dark and grungy
than her comics counterpart, only cleaning

00:09:51.560 --> 00:09:54.690
up a bit when her powers are at full force.

00:09:54.690 --> 00:10:00.670
The onscreen Enchantress, played by Cara Delevingne,
wears a hunter green swimsuit and crescent

00:10:00.670 --> 00:10:03.750
headdress, a weird shadow of her comics counterpart.

00:10:03.750 --> 00:10:09.750
And, for some reason, the filmmakers decided
to make her look kinda like Pigpen from Charlie

00:10:09.750 --> 00:10:10.750
Brown.

00:10:10.750 --> 00:10:11.779
Good grief.

00:10:11.779 --> 00:10:12.779
Thanks for watching!

00:10:12.779 --> 00:10:16.670
Subscribe to our YouTube channel to watch
more videos like the one you just saw.

00:10:16.670 --> 00:10:20.759
And leave us a comment to let us know which
costume upgrade you like the most…

